<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Sánchez of Gascony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 997</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Sánchez was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> from about 961 until about 997. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders, traditionally at the Battle of Taller around 982, founded the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sever">Saint-Sever</a> in 988 and, after inheriting the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, made it the ducal seat. Through his marriage to Urraca of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> he had close ties with the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Sánchez, known in Basque as Gilen Antso and in Gascon as Guilhem Sans, was the younger illegitimate son of Duke Sancho IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. Around 961 he succeeded his childless elder brother, Duke Sancho V. Documents of his reign state that his grandfather came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, which lends some credence to genealogies placing the origins of García II Sánchez across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Around 970 he still called himself only count, as when he gave land at Luco Deo to the monastery of Saint Vincent-de-Lucq.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He inherited the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> from his cousin William the Good and joined it permanently to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>; before 977 he also added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agenais">Agenais</a> and the Bazadais. In 977 he restored the priory of La Réole as dux Wasconum, and by 988 he held Bordeaux, where he consulted its lords on the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sever">Saint-Sever</a>. Aimoin of Fleury called him count of Bordeaux and duke of all Gascony. Because Bordeaux lay within the orbit of the French kings, he began dating his charters by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>.</p><p>His brother Gombald, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of the Gascons, gained control of every Gascon diocese and became Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, so that the family held the whole church hierarchy. William left Gombald in charge when he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> to fight the Muslims, but returned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids grew serious. After his victory at Taller the Vikings ceased to be a serious threat, their settlements along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> were removed, and they were driven north into the pays de Born-Landes. He may also be the Count William Sánchez who, according to Sampiro, defeated Vikings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in 970, perhaps while on pilgrimage to Compostela, though no other record places him there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William married Urraca Garcés, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-i-of-pamplona">García Sánchez I of Pamplona</a> and widow of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González of Castile</a>. He was succeeded by his son Bernard William, and another son, Sancho VI, later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> too. His daughter Brisca married Duke William V of Aquitaine and brought a claim on Gascony to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">House of Poitiers</a>. The growth of the duchy under William is probably why more is known of the region after a period of obscurity that had lasted since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids of the 840s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Sánchez of Gascony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>William Sánchez</b> defeated Viking raiders and founded the abbey of Saint-Sever, and Bordeaux became the ducal seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-gascony">Duchy of Gascony</a></li></ul></section>
